Make OGDF elderberry-202309 build for wasm32-unknown-emscripten.

**Already applied to `vendor/ogdf`.** The build does not run this; it is the
record of how that tree differs from the upstream tag, and what to reapply when
bumping to a newer one. See vendor/README.md.

1. `-march=native` is fatal, not merely useless. OGDF adds it for any compiler
   identifying as GNU or Clang, and emcc identifies as Clang, so a stock
   checkout fails outright:

     clang++: error: unsupported option '-march=' for target
              'wasm32-unknown-emscripten'

   Dropping Clang from the guard is also the right answer for a COMMITTED
   artifact even where the flag is accepted: `native` means "whatever CPU ran
   the build", which is the opposite of a byte-reproducible engine.

   This is the patch BandageNG carries in its own vendored OGDF, arrived at
   independently and kept identical on purpose — it is what the engine has been
   built against since it was ported, so it is also what every committed
   force-directed figure was drawn with.

2. `<chrono>` is used but not included. Newer libc++ stopped pulling it in
   transitively, so the file fails to compile. Nothing in the layout path calls
   it; it is in the same translation unit set, so it still has to build.

--- a/cmake/compiler-specifics.cmake
+++ b/cmake/compiler-specifics.cmake
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@
 endif()

 # use native arch (ie, activate things like SSE)
-if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "GNU|Clang" AND NOT ${CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR} MATCHES "^arm")
+if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "GNU" AND NOT ${CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR} MATCHES "^arm")
   # cannot use add_definitions() here because it does not work with check-sse3.cmake
   set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -march=native")
 endif()
--- a/src/ogdf/cluster/HananiTutteCPlanarity.cpp
+++ b/src/ogdf/cluster/HananiTutteCPlanarity.cpp
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include <ogdf/cluster/ClusterPlanarity.h>
 #include <ogdf/cluster/HananiTutteCPlanarity.h>

+#include <chrono>
 #include <map>
 #include <unordered_map>

